{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context\n\nA ticket can carry so many acceptance criteria that one completion-verification run strains to\ncover them all, and nothing usefully warns the author. The motivating case was a 14-AC ticket\n(`9fd4`) that produced no size signal at all.\n\n**What already exists — established by inspection on 2026-08-09, and it changes this ticket's\nshape.** `p4_oversize` in `src/rebar/llm/plan_review/det_floor.py:366-403` is a DET check that\nalready fires on ALL tickets (no leaf scoping), is already advisory and never blocks, and already\ncarries THREE size dimensions, not one:\n\n- `P4_AC_SOFT_CAP = 25` checklist items (`_count_ac_items`, :169 — counts both `- [ ]` and `- [x]`\n  under an exact `## Acceptance Criteria` heading, via `evaluate_plan_clarity`)\n- `P4_FILE_IMPACT_SOFT_CAP = 30` file-impact entries\n- `P4_DESC_SOFT_CAP_CHARS = 24_000` description characters\n\nTwo consequences:\n\n1. **The description-length dimension already exists.** It does not need to be added as a new DET\n   criterion.\n2. **The duplicate-reporting concern is already solved structurally.** All three signals accumulate\n   into a single `signals` list inside ONE `DetResult` finding, so a long description and a high AC\n   count produce one finding with two evidence lines — not two competing findings. Building a\n   suppression mechanism between a new description-length criterion and `p4_oversize` would add\n   machinery to solve a problem the current design does not have. Keep both dimensions inside P4.\n\nSo the real defects are calibration and a missing quality dimension:\n\n- **The AC cap is hardcoded at 25 and was never derived from data.** It is far above the observed\n  pain point — it would NOT have fired on the 14-AC ticket that motivated this work.\n- **Nothing judges AC QUALITY, only AC COUNT.** A count-only signal punishes thoroughness. In this\n  repo the same nominal count spans wildly different weights: some criteria are compound and\n  detailed (multiple independent assertions, several verification commands, in one checkbox), while\n  others are vacuous — restating things already verified outside the AC system entirely (\"children\n  are complete\", \"tests pass\", \"CI is green\", \"lint passes\"). Counting those two the same is what\n  makes a pure count a poor proxy for \"this ticket should be split\".\n\nConfirmed absent today (searched `criteria_routing.json`, `docs/plan-review-criteria-guide.md`, and\nevery `src/rebar/llm/reviewers/plan_review_*.md`):\n\n- No criterion flags a VACUOUS acceptance criterion. `P1` records `empty_ac_items` (literally blank\n  items) and `F1` flags effort-framed / subjective / post-sprint-only criteria, but nothing flags a\n  criterion that is measurable yet redundant with a gate that already runs.\n- No criterion flags a NON-ATOMIC acceptance criterion. `F1`'s right-sizing check (d) is about the\n  UNIT being a coherent single outcome, not about an individual criterion bundling several\n  independent assertions.\n\nThe natural home is the existing `ac-text-quality` dimension, which today holds exactly three\ncriteria: `E2` (ambiguity), `E6` (verification/end-state reachability), `F1` (measurability &\nin-session completability).\n\n## Plan\n\n**Part A — calibrate the count from history, do not guess it.** Replay the persisted\n`*-REVIEW_RESULT.json` sidecar corpus under `.tickets-tracker/` (the zero-LLM-cost instrument\nticket `f391` established) together with the ticket store, and find where AC count actually starts\npredicting trouble — tickets that were later split, reopened, or whose completion verification had\nto shard or recover. Report the distribution, not just a number, and pick the soft cap from that\ncurve. Make the chosen cap config-driven rather than a module constant so it can be retuned without\na code change. Do the same for the description-length cap while the instrument is in hand.\n\nThe goal is right-sized tickets, NOT punishing thorough authors: prefer a cap that fires where\nsplitting genuinely helps, and accept false negatives over false positives on an advisory signal.\n\n**Part B — add the missing quality dimension.** Add ONE criterion under the existing\n`ac-text-quality` dimension that produces ADVISORY guidance on criterion quality:\n\n- flag a VACUOUS criterion — one whose truth is already established by a gate that runs regardless\n  (children closed, tests pass, CI green, lint/typecheck clean) — and advise dropping it, since it\n  inflates the count while proving nothing the ticket is responsible for;\n- flag a NON-ATOMIC criterion — one bundling several independently-verifiable assertions — and\n  advise splitting it into separate criteria. Detail is explicitly GOOD; the finding is about\n  non-atomic complexity, not about length or thoroughness.\n\nBoth are advisory and must never block a claim. Weigh extending `F1` against adding a sibling\ncriterion in the same dimension, and record which was chosen and why — `F1` is already a dense\nmulti-part rubric, so overloading it may cost detection quality.\n\n**Part C — do not build suppression machinery.** Keep description length inside `p4_oversize`. If\nPart B's criterion and P4 can both fire on the same ticket, that is acceptable and non-duplicative:\nP4 says \"this unit looks big\", Part B says \"these specific criteria are weak\". They are different\nclaims. Only if measurement shows them firing together on the same ROOT cause should suppression be\nconsidered, and then it should be filed separately.\n\n## Testing\n\nPart A's calibration is an analysis: verification is that the distribution and the chosen cap are\nreproducible from the stated corpus, with the denominator stated.\n\nPart B needs criterion tests in the established pattern for a routed criterion: fixtures with a\nvacuous criterion, a non-atomic criterion, and — critically — a NEGATIVE control that is long,\ndetailed and thorough but genuinely atomic and non-vacuous, which must PASS. Without that control\nthe criterion cannot be shown to avoid punishing thoroughness.\n\nRetuning `P4_AC_SOFT_CAP` must not change P4's advisory posture: a test must pin that P4 still never\nblocks, and that its three signals still produce a single accumulated finding.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] The AC-count soft cap is derived from a replay of historical tickets, with the distribution\n      and the chosen threshold both recorded on this ticket, including the denominator.\n- [ ] The AC-count soft cap and the description-length soft cap are config-driven rather than\n      module constants, and their defaults are the derived values.\n- [ ] The description-length dimension remains inside `p4_oversize` and no separate description\n      length criterion is added, so the two size signals continue to accumulate into one finding.\n- [ ] `p4_oversize` continues to fire on ALL tickets (not leaf-scoped) and remains advisory —\n      pinned by a test asserting it never blocks.\n- [ ] A criterion under the `ac-text-quality` dimension flags a VACUOUS acceptance criterion (one\n      already established by a gate that runs regardless) and advises dropping it.\n- [ ] The same dimension flags a NON-ATOMIC acceptance criterion (several independently-verifiable\n      assertions in one item) and advises splitting it.\n- [ ] The decision to extend `F1` versus add a sibling criterion is recorded with its reasoning.\n- [ ] A negative-control fixture that is long, detailed, and thorough but atomic and non-vacuous\n      PASSES the new criterion, demonstrating thoroughness is not penalized.\n- [ ] The new criterion is registered in `criteria_routing.json` and documented in\n      `docs/plan-review-criteria-guide.md`, and is readable via `rebar explain <criterion-id>`.\n- [ ] The new criterion is advisory and cannot block a claim.","title":"Right-size tickets: calibrate P4 oversize caps from history and add AC-quality advisories"}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1786326532809047001,"uuid":"04d41aa6-bb23-4b3a-b2dd-9fc359c28727"}